\subsection{Does the direction of the perturbation matter?}

Random noise breaks the symmetry in an arbitrary direction. A better
direction seems available: the residual of the log-likelihood with respect to
the leaf's distribution says which classes the leaf under-predicts, and the
two children can be placed at $Q_\ell \pm \varepsilon d$ along that unit
direction $d$, with the same expected perturbation size as the noise. The new
gate can also be pointed at the samples whose residual aligns with $+d$, so
that routing starts informative. This is GradMax's idea \cite{evci2022gradmax}
applied to a tree gate, and it is implemented as \texttt{growth\_init} in the
library. Table~\ref{tab:direction} compares it with random noise on all
twenty-four datasets.

