The size effect is consistent and the accuracy effect is not, and I want to be
exact about which is which.

Every dataset gives a smaller network. Iris falls from @@T_G_IRIS_RND_H@@ hidden
units to @@T_G_IRIS_RES_H@@, Wine from @@T_G_WINE_RND_H@@ to @@T_G_WINE_RES_H@@,
Digits from @@T_G_DIGITS_RND_H@@ to @@T_G_DIGITS_RES_H@@. The mechanism is
visible in the growth trace. A random unit arrives unhelpful, the error does not
fall, and the growth criterion fires again, so the network grows because growth
is not working. Fitting the unit to the residual first removes that loop.

Accuracy goes the other way, and on the largest dataset it goes there
significantly. It rose on Iris and was identical on Wine to three decimals, and
the $F$ test rejects equality on neither. On Digits it fell by
@@GAL_DIGITS_DROP@@ points and the $F$ test does reject equality, in favour of
random initialisation.

So the operation is a parsimony intervention that costs accuracy on the one
dataset here with enough classes and features to strain it. The likely reason is
visible in the same table: the residual variant stops at @@T_G_DIGITS_RES_H@@
hidden units where the random variant keeps going to @@T_G_DIGITS_RND_H@@, and on
a ten-class problem those extra units are doing work. Growth that stops early is
parsimony on an easy problem and underfitting on a harder one, and the growth
criterion cannot tell the two apart.

An earlier draft of this work reported the Iris accuracy gain on its own. That is
the kind of claim that survives exactly until somebody runs a fourth dataset.

On the twenty OpenML datasets the picture is the same. The residual variant
gives a smaller network on average, @@OM_GAL_RATIO@@\% fewer hidden units, and
against random initialisation it wins on @@OM_GAL_WIN@@, ties on
@@OM_GAL_TIE@@ and loses on @@OM_GAL_LOSS@@ by more than half a point.

One implementation detail cost me a day. The candidate objective involves a
normalisation of the form $s / \sqrt{v}$ where $v$ is a variance that can be zero
on a degenerate batch. At $v = 0$ the derivative of the square root is infinite
and the whole network becomes \texttt{NaN} in one step. Clamping $v$ away from
zero before the root fixes it. Clamping the result afterwards does not, because
by then the gradient has already been formed.

\section{Which leaf to split}

A soft tree can be grown a level at a time, as in Section~3, or a leaf at a
time. The per-leaf rule of \.Irsoy, Y\i{}ld\i{}z and Alpayd\i{}n
\cite{irsoy2012soft} splits the leaf carrying the most expected error,
$\arg\max_\ell \sum_i \mu_\ell(x_i)\,e_i$, where $e_i$ is the error on sample
$i$. It produces by far the sparsest trees.

\subsection{The same defect in another place}

On the twenty OpenML datasets, per-leaf growth against a complete depth-six
tree won on @@OM_PL_WIN@@, tied on @@OM_PL_TIE@@ and lost on @@OM_PL_LOSS@@,
by @@OM_PL_GAP@@ points on average, and it lost on every multi-class dataset.
The sparsity was real, from 63 splits to between one and eighteen, but it
looked as if it were paid for in accuracy.

It was paid for in a defect. When a leaf was split, the implementation marked
the node as split and did nothing else. Its two children kept the untrained
logits they were constructed with, so the split replaced the parent's learned
distribution with a uniform one and started the children identical to each
other. That is Proposition~\ref{prop:zero} again, at a single node instead of
a whole level: no gradient for the new gate, and the tree's function broken
at every split for good measure. The level-wise deepening of Section~3 had
been cured of both problems; the per-leaf path had not.

The fix is the one Section~3 uses. The children inherit the parent's logits,
the gate is reset to neutral, and the symmetry is broken by noise or along the
leaf's residual direction. Table~\ref{tab:perleaf} measures the difference on
Digits and the seven multi-class OpenML datasets, which are where the loss
was. The column marked 90\% is the complete tree trained with the same 10\%
holdout the grown tree uses, so that the two can be compared on equal data.

